\title{Fixing a terminological mistake}
\taxon{Changelog}
\date{2026-11-26}
\author{samueltoth}

\p{This entry is regarding an extremely small change to the library that I nonetheless
wanted to highlight. The changelog has been quiet lately as I recently started my
PhD studies at the [University of Nottingham](unott) under the supervision
of [Nicolai Kraus](nicolaikraus). I have been working a little in the background
on some boring, but much needed refactorings and cleanups of the
library, and [Fredrik Bakke](fredrikbakke) and [Mark Williams](markwilliams) have
been working on some much more exciting formalisations, which will hopefully be
merged soon. The change in question relates to the following definition: }

\transclude{stt-00LD}

\p{We had previously used the terminology \em{triangulated} for this notion.
This isn't a very good choice for a number of reasons.

Because there is an inclusion of the walking triangulated square into the square,
every square in a type naturally gives rise to a triangulated square
in that type by composition. Being \em{square complete} gives you an inverse
to this map, and so a square complete type should be thought of as being
a type where one is able to glue together a pair of compatible triangles into
a square. Naturally, the triangulated square is not square complete (if it were
then every type wolud be); this fact sounds quite non-sensical with our previous
choice of terminology, and this gets even worse when you take \em{"triangulation"}
to be the name of the modal operator: "the triangulation of the triangulated
square is the square".
}

\p{I apologise for any confusion to which this naming choice may have contributed.}
